WEBVTT

00:00:00.270 --> 00:00:03.000
<v Ira Glass>From WBEZ Chicago, it's This American Life.
<v Ira Glass>来自芝加哥WBEZ电台，这里是《美国生活》。

00:00:03.000 --> 00:00:04.580
<v Ira Glass>OK, give me a level.
<v Ira Glass>好的，给我调下音量。

00:00:04.580 --> 00:00:09.060
<v Neil Drumming>This is me about how much, how loud I talk.
<v Neil Drumming>这是我说话大概的音量，多大声。

00:00:09.060 --> 00:00:13.730
<v Ira Glass>Not long ago, one of my coworkers here at the radio show, Neil Drumming, and I took a field trip to Philadelphia.
<v Ira Glass>不久前，我和我们广播节目的同事尼尔·德拉明（Neil Drumming）去了一趟费城。

00:00:13.730 --> 00:00:18.100
<v Neil Drumming>We're going to Amalgam Comics and Coffee.
<v Neil Drumming>我们要去Amalgam漫画咖啡馆。

00:00:18.100 --> 00:00:19.310
<v Ira Glass>It's a comic shop.
<v Ira Glass>那是一家漫画店。

00:00:19.310 --> 00:00:22.020
<v Neil Drumming>It's a comic book store. Yeah, and also, like, a coffee shop.
<v Neil Drumming>那是一家漫画书店。没错，同时也是一家咖啡馆。

00:00:22.020 --> 00:00:23.420
<v Ira Glass>No, no. I got that from the name.
<v Ira Glass>不，不。我从名字就看出来了。

00:00:23.420 --> 00:00:32.510
<v Neil Drumming>[CHUCKLING] Yes. But it's, I think, the first comic book store owned by a black woman on the East Coast.
<v Neil Drumming>[轻笑] 是的。但我认为它是东海岸第一家由黑人女性拥有的漫画书店。

00:00:32.510 --> 00:00:34.010
<v Ira Glass>On the East Coast? We have to add that?
<v Ira Glass>在东海岸？我们必须加上这个限定吗？

00:00:34.010 --> 00:00:35.530
<v Neil Drumming>I think we do.
<v Neil Drumming>我想我们得加上。

00:00:35.530 --> 00:00:48.350
<v Ira Glass>We're here because recently Neil has gotten really interested in Afrofuturism. And there's this whole intersection between Afrofuturism and comic books. The founder of something called the Afrofuturism Network is speaking at this comic store on this particular day.
<v Ira Glass>我们来这里是因为尼尔最近对“非洲未来主义”（Afrofuturism）非常感兴趣。非洲未来主义和漫画书之间存在着某种交集。今天，所谓的“非洲未来主义网络”的创始人在就在这家漫画店演讲。

00:00:48.350 --> 00:00:52.040
<v Ira Glass>And what exactly is Afrofuturism? Neil's been prepping for this question.
<v Ira Glass>究竟什么是非洲未来主义？尼尔一直在为这个问题做准备。

00:00:52.040 --> 00:01:25.270
<v Neil Drumming>I don't know. There's-- there's, like, a long-winded academic Wikipedia explanation, which I've never even been able to internalize or remember, honestly, if we're being honest. What I like about Afrofuturism, it just seems very-- it's like this way of talking about black people in a way that's really hopeful. It's this idea that we would be engaged in the same kinds of things that, you know, science fiction writers have always talked about-- that we would be engaged with technology, that we would have a future in space, that we would master time travel, that we would have a future in Utopian or Dystopian landscapes. It's like--
<v Neil Drumming>我不知道。呃……有一种冗长的学术性维基百科解释，老实说，如果坦诚一点的话，我从来都没能真正理解或记住它。我喜欢非洲未来主义的地方在于，它看起来非常……这是一种以真正充满希望的方式谈论黑人的方式。它传达了这样一种理念：我们会参与到那些科幻小说作家一直谈论的事情中——我们会参与科技，我们在太空中拥有未来，我们将掌握时间旅行，我们将在乌托邦或反乌托邦的景观中拥有未来。这就像……

00:01:25.270 --> 00:01:53.950
<v Ira Glass>So partly this is about science fiction. That's really just the tip of the iceberg. And the science fiction part is meaningful because, while there have been black characters in sci-fi for a while now, they're almost never the protagonists. They're never the ones driving the action. And for so long, in so many imaginings of the future, in so much science fiction, there were no black people at all-- which, as Neil points out, makes no sense. He says, you can tell black people are going to make it into the future because they've survived so much already over the centuries.
<v Ira Glass>所以这部分与科幻小说有关。那其实只是冰山一角。科幻小说部分之所以有意义，是因为虽然科幻作品中已经有一段时间出现黑人角色了，但他们几乎从不是主角。他们从未主导剧情。在很长一段时间里，在对未来的许多想象中，在太多的科幻小说中，根本就没有黑人——正如尼尔指出的那样，这完全没道理。他说，你可以看出黑人注定会走向未来，因为几个世纪以来他们已经在如此多的困境中存活了下来。

00:01:53.950 --> 00:02:12.740
<v Neil Drumming>One of the things that's sort of really specific about Afrofuturism that I like is that it takes into account the past in a lot of ways. It imagines that, you know, black people have forms of survival through, you know, the slave trade, through persecution, that that's almost a technology in itself, the ways in which we've come through those things.
<v Neil Drumming>非洲未来主义中我特别喜欢的一点是，它在很多方面都考虑到了过去。它设想……你知道，黑人通过奴隶贸易、通过迫害所形成的生存方式，本身几乎就是一种技术，是我们熬过这些苦难的方式。

00:02:12.740 --> 00:02:14.220
<v Ira Glass>Oh, I see. The survival skills.
<v Ira Glass>哦，我明白了。生存技能。

00:02:14.220 --> 00:02:49.720
<v Neil Drumming>Yeah. And that everything that's been a part of our culture has made it possible for us to pave the way for the future. That's the thing that I like about it, I think-- the feeling like, despite whatever trials or travails you've come through, that you will exist in the future. That just kind of makes me-- it makes me kind of, like, love being black. It makes me feel what I love about being black-- like, oh yeah, you know, we can get through this.
<v Neil Drumming>是的。我们文化中所有的一切都使我们能够为未来铺平道路。我想这就是我喜欢它的地方——尽管经历了无论什么样的磨难和艰辛，你都会在未来占有一席之地，这种感觉。这让我……这让我有点，就像，爱上身为黑人。这让我感受到我热爱身为黑人的原因——就像，哦是的，你知道，我们可以渡过难关。

00:02:49.720 --> 00:02:59.920
<v Ira Glass>Things I've known about forever, Neil says, are now called Afrofuturism. Old-school stuff, like Parliament-Funkadelic's pimped-out trippy rides to outer space, which predates the term by 20 years.
<v Ira Glass>尼尔说，我一直都知道的事情，现在被称为非洲未来主义。老派的东西，比如Parliament-Funkadelic乐队那种打扮得花里胡哨、飞往外太空的迷幻之旅，这种概念比这个词的出现早了20年。

00:02:59.920 --> 00:03:11.180
<v George Clinton>(RAPPING) All right, Starchild. Citizens of the universe, recording angels, we have returned to claim the pyramids. Partying on the mothership. I am the mothership connection.
<v George Clinton>（饶舌）好的，星之子。宇宙的公民们，记录天使们，我们已归来夺回金字塔。在母舰上狂欢。我是母舰连接者。

00:03:11.180 --> 00:03:32.560
<v Ira Glass>And lately you see lots of famous black people excited about Afrofuturism. Rihanna and Solange appear in black space-age fashion. The director of Selma and 13th, Ava Duvernay, is making a TV series based on a novel by the woman who is kind of a godmother to the movement-- sci-fi writer Octavia Butler. That's after Duvernay finishes the science fiction classic A Wrinkle in Time, with black actors in lots of the leads.
<v Ira Glass>最近你可以看到很多著名的黑人对非洲未来主义感到兴奋。蕾哈娜（Rihanna）和索兰格（Solange）穿着黑人太空时代的时尚服饰出现。《塞尔玛》（Selma）和《第13修正案》（13th）的导演阿娃·杜威内（Ava DuVernay）正在制作一部根据一位女性小说家的书改编的电视剧，这位作家算是该运动的教母——科幻作家奥克塔维亚·巴特勒（Octavia Butler）。在那之前，杜威内刚刚完成了科幻经典《时间的皱折》（A Wrinkle in Time），其中大量主演都是黑人演员。

00:03:32.560 --> 00:03:43.810
<v Ira Glass>Donald Glover's last Childish Gambino album was a tribute to Parliament-Funkadelic. And Janelle Monae's tour a couple of years ago was a whole sci-fi stage show with men in lab coats and a story about androids. She sings about spaceships.
<v Ira Glass>唐纳德·格洛弗（Donald Glover）的最后一张Childish Gambino专辑是对Parliament-Funkadelic的致敬。而加奈儿·梦奈（Janelle Monae）几年前的巡演是一场完整的科幻舞台秀，里面有穿着实验服的人和关于机器人的故事。她歌唱着宇宙飞船。

00:03:43.810 --> 00:03:56.100
<v Janelle Monae>(SINGING) Baby, ooh. l'll reprogram your mind. Come on, get in. My spaceship leaves at 10. Oh!
<v Janelle Monae>（唱歌）宝贝，哦。我会重新编程你的大脑。来吧，进来。我的飞船10点出发。哦！

00:03:56.100 --> 00:04:10.890
<v Ira Glass>And comics-- Neil and I were in Philadelphia for comics, after all. The thing that Neil was most excited to see in the comic book store was the new Iron Man, where-- I don't know if you've heard this-- in the comics, Tony Stark is in a coma, replaced by a black teenage girl, Genius.
<v Ira Glass>还有漫画——毕竟，我和尼尔是为了漫画才在费城的。尼尔在漫画店里最兴奋看到的东西是新的《钢铁侠》，因为……我不知道你听说过没有……在漫画里，托尼·斯塔克（Tony Stark）陷入了昏迷，取而代之的是一个黑人少女，天才少女。

00:04:10.890 --> 00:04:17.610
<v Neil Drumming>And she invents a suit that's comparable to Tony Stark's Iron Man suit, which is, like, my favorite piece of fictional technology anyway.
<v Neil Drumming>她发明了一套可以媲美托尼·斯塔克钢铁侠战甲的装备，这本来就是我最喜欢的虚构科技。

00:04:17.610 --> 00:04:25.980
<v Ira Glass>As he points out, replacing Tony Stark with a black teenage girl, that pierces right into the Arc Reactor heart of the techno superhero sci-fi pantheon.
<v Ira Glass>正如他指出的，用一个黑人少女替换托尼·斯塔克，直接击中了科技超级英雄科幻万神殿那方舟反应堆般的核心。

00:04:25.980 --> 00:04:28.820
<v Neil Drumming>I mean, think about who Tony Stark, the original Iron Man, is.
<v Neil Drumming>我的意思是，想想最初的钢铁侠托尼·斯塔克是谁。

00:04:28.820 --> 00:04:29.550
<v Ira Glass>Right.
<v Ira Glass>没错。

00:04:29.550 --> 00:04:42.500
<v Neil Drumming>He's a wealthy white man. He's, like, a millionaire white guy. He's the unattainable. Like, it's great to have a robot suit and save the world, but how do most people imagine themselves in that context?
<v Neil Drumming>他是个富有的白人。他是个百万富翁白人。他是高不可攀的存在。拥有一套机器人战甲并拯救世界固然很棒，但大多数人如何在那种情境下想象自己呢？

00:04:42.500 --> 00:04:42.990
<v Ira Glass>Yeah.
<v Ira Glass>是的。

00:04:42.990 --> 00:04:52.270
<v Neil Drumming>Whereas this girl, her education takes her to this place that a billionaire once was at. Like, that's-- I mean, that's inspiring. It's moving.
<v Neil Drumming>而这个女孩，她的教育带她达到了曾经只有亿万富翁才能达到的高度。这……我的意思是，这很鼓舞人心。这很动人。

00:04:52.270 --> 00:05:12.180
<v Neil Drumming>I don't know. Maybe I'm being-- I've been thinking about it a lot. And like, why has my interest been sparked so much recently? I've always loved science fiction, right? But the idea that, like-- the idea of black people in science fiction appeals to me now because of that notion of telling your own story, because the way black people are talked about, it's often a bleak future.
<v Neil Drumming>我不知道。也许我……我最近一直在想这个问题。为什么我最近的兴趣被点燃了这么多？我一直很喜欢科幻小说，对吧？但黑人在科幻小说中的概念现在吸引我，是因为那种讲述自己故事的理念，因为黑人被谈论的方式，通常是一个凄凉的未来。

00:05:12.180 --> 00:05:13.460
<v Ira Glass>And a bleak present.
<v Ira Glass>还有一个凄凉的现状。

00:05:13.460 --> 00:05:17.860
<v Neil Drumming>Yeah, yeah. A bleak present and maybe no future at all, you know what I mean?
<v Neil Drumming>是的，是的。一个凄凉的现在，也许根本就没有未来，你懂我的意思吗？

00:05:17.860 --> 00:05:18.260
<v Ira Glass>Yeah.
<v Ira Glass>是的。

00:05:18.260 --> 00:05:38.130
<v Neil Drumming>And so, I mean, think about-- I don't know. I mean, I don't-- maybe not everyone feels this. But I've been watching. There's just a lot of videos of terrible things happening to black people. I mean, it's literally, like, almost a genre. And there's got to be a parallel narrative, you know what I mean? There's got to be a parallel narrative.
<v Neil Drumming>所以，我的意思是，想想……我不知道。我是说，我不……也许并不是每个人都有这种感觉。但我一直在观察。真的有很多关于黑人遭遇可怕事情的视频。我的意思是，这真的几乎成了一种体裁。必须有一个平行的叙事，你懂我的意思吗？必须有一个平行的叙事。

00:05:38.130 --> 00:05:59.200
<v Neil Drumming>And I think for me, the parallel narrative is Afrofuturism. And so, I don't know, it's a way of feeling like-- it's so hard for me to say this without laughing at myself. But it is genuinely hopeful. It's like a way of feeling hope.
<v Neil Drumming>我认为对我来说，那个平行的叙事就是非洲未来主义。所以，我不知道，这是一种感觉像……如果不嘲笑自己，我很难说出这句话。但它确实是发自内心的充满希望。这就像一种获得希望的方式。

00:05:59.200 --> 00:06:22.650
<v Ira Glass>We sit on a curb way too long talking about this and finally head inside the comic bookstore. It's bright and spacious and smells great, like fresh coffee, decked out in posters for Wonder Woman and Spidey and X-Men and Moongirl. The store was busy because Afrofuturism enthusiast and author William Jones was there to give a lecture on the history of black people in comic books. He popped a slide of a comic book cover from the '60s onto the screen
<v Ira Glass>我们在路边坐了太久来讨论这件事，最后走进那家漫画书店。店里明亮宽敞，闻起来很棒，有新鲜咖啡的味道，到处都是《神奇女侠》、《蜘蛛侠》、《X战警》和《月亮女孩》的海报。店里很热闹，因为非洲未来主义爱好者、作家威廉·琼斯（William Jones）当时正在那里举办关于漫画书中黑人历史的讲座。他在屏幕上放了一张60年代漫画书封面的幻灯片。

00:06:22.650 --> 00:06:43.200
<v William Jones>This is 1966. Now we have the introduction of the Black Panther-- not affiliated with the political organization. And the reason why I say that is because Marvel Comics recognized that maybe people are going to think we support the Black Panther Party. So they briefly change his name to the Black Leopard. It didn't last, and folks were not buying it.
<v William Jones>这是1966年。现在我们迎来了黑豹（Black Panther）的登场——与那个政治组织无关。我之所以这么说，是因为漫威漫画意识到人们可能会认为他们支持黑豹党。所以他们短暂地将他的名字改成了黑豹（Black Leopard）。这没持续多久，而且大家并不买账。

00:06:43.200 --> 00:07:06.220
<v Ira Glass>Most of the crowd is black. And nearly every adult Neil and I talked to was familiar with the basic ideas and iconic figures of Afrofuturism, including the owner, Ariell Johnson, a super-energetic 34. She and Neil talked about the subject for a while, and it became clear that for Neil, Afrofuturism is really about the future. But for Ariell, it's about the present, something she sees everyday.
<v Ira Glass>大多数听众是黑人。尼尔和我交谈的几乎每一位成年人都熟悉非洲未来主义的基本理念和标志性人物，包括店主阿里尔·约翰逊（Ariell Johnson），一位非常有活力的34岁女性。她和尼尔就这个话题聊了一会儿，很明显，对尼尔来说，非洲未来主义真正关乎未来。但对阿里尔来说，它关乎现在，是她每天都能看到的东西。

00:07:06.220 --> 00:07:19.360
<v Ariell Johnson>Black people today, black people who are alive and walking around, it's like, we are Afrofuturism. There's a quote that says, "I am my ancestors' wildest dreams." And I think that's true.
<v Ariell Johnson>今天的黑人，活着的、走动着的黑人，就像是，我们就是非洲未来主义。有一句名言说：“我是我祖先最狂野的梦。”我认为那是真的。

00:07:19.360 --> 00:07:41.310
<v Ariell Johnson>It's like, when we think about our history in this country from when we were first brought here and the condition that they were in, you know, the freedoms that we have now, the things that we are working to accomplish and achieve, those are only things that they could have dreamed about in their wildest dreams. They could not have imagined-- it's like, I own this store, you know what I mean?
<v Ariell Johnson>就像，当我们想到我们在这个国家的历史，从我们第一次被带到这里，以及当时人们所处的境况，再看我们现在拥有的自由，我们努力去完成和实现的事情，这些都只是他们敢在最狂野的梦里想象的事情。他们无法想象……就像，我拥有这家店，你懂我的意思吗？

00:07:41.310 --> 00:07:52.500
<v Ariell Johnson>So I am Afrofuturism. You are Afrofuturism. The fact that we're walking around, we are defying and challenging stereotypes and negative images.
<v Ariell Johnson>所以我就是非洲未来主义。你也是非洲未来主义。我们正走来走去的事实，就是在蔑视和挑战刻板印象与负面形象。

00:07:52.500 --> 00:08:08.940
<v Ira Glass>It's been a hard week for stereotypes and negative images. And it's been a brutal week for the past returning into the present. Mobs of men carrying torches, people arguing about Confederate statues and George Washington's slaves. It's been a week where lots of us have wondered about the future in this country and where things are going.
<v Ira Glass>这对刻板印象和负面形象来说是艰难的一周。这对过去重返现实来说也是残酷的一周。拿着火把的暴徒，人们争论着南方邦联雕像和乔治·华盛顿的奴隶。在这一周里，我们许多人都对自己在这个国家的未来以及事态的走向感到担忧。

00:08:08.940 --> 00:08:19.320
<v Ira Glass>Against that, today, we pose another way to see the world, another way to look at the future and the past that puts it in some context and helps us make sense of things, maybe gives us a little hope.
<v Ira Glass>与之相对，今天，我们提出了另一种看待世界的方式，另一种审视未来和过去的方式，它将其置于一定的背景下，帮助我们理解事物，也许还能给我们一点希望。

00:08:19.320 --> 00:08:21.960
<v Ira Glass>I'm joined in the studio right now by my colleague. Neil, hello.
<v Ira Glass>我现在在演播室里和我的同事在一起。尼尔，你好。

00:08:21.960 --> 00:08:22.530
<v Neil Drumming>Hey.
<v Neil Drumming>嘿。

00:08:22.530 --> 00:08:30.300
<v Ira Glass>Hey. OK, so my thinking is-- well, our thinking is, since I know really very little about this, about Afrofuturism, and you know, like--
<v Ira Glass>嘿。好吧，我的想法是……或者说我们的想法是，因为我对这个——非洲未来主义——了解得实在太少，而你知道，就像……

00:08:30.300 --> 00:08:32.030
<v Neil Drumming>I think I know a little bit more, quite a bit more.
<v Neil Drumming>我想我知道得多一点，多得多。

00:08:32.030 --> 00:08:38.550
<v Ira Glass>OK. So our plan is that I'm going to go away, at least till halfway through the show, and you will stick around.
<v Ira Glass>好的。所以我们的计划是，我会离开，至少直到节目中段，而你会留下来。

00:08:38.550 --> 00:08:45.420
<v Neil Drumming>Yeah. No, I've just been-- I've been writing pieces and talking to people all over the country about this. So I feel like I have a little bit of a grasp on it.
<v Neil Drumming>是的。不，我只是……我一直在撰写文章，并与全国各地的人们交流这件事。所以我感觉我对此有一点点把握。

00:08:45.420 --> 00:08:53.070
<v Ira Glass>OK. All right, so I'll see you halfway through. Till then, I'm going to throw some sci-fi here. You have the conn.
<v Ira Glass>好的。好吧，那我们中段见。在那之前，我要抛出一些科幻内容了。指挥权交给你。

00:08:53.070 --> 00:09:01.310
<v Neil Drumming>I appreciate that. All right, stay with us.
<v Neil Drumming>我很感激。好的，请留在我们身边。

00:09:01.310 --> 00:09:29.640
<v Neil Drumming>Act One, "Metropolis Now." I started really puzzling over Afrofuturism a couple of years ago, when I went to an Afrofuturism conference in New York. Up until that day, I'd mostly heard the term used to refer to musicians like Sun Ra, or to science fiction by Samuel Delany or Colson Whitehead. But at this conference, there were Afrofuturism artists, designers, programmers, filmmakers, fashionistas, healers, yoginis. There was even a woman selling homemade hot pepper sauce in the lobby.
<v Neil Drumming>第一幕，“大都会现在”。几年前，我在纽约参加了一次非洲未来主义会议，当时我开始真正琢磨起这个话题。在那一天之前，我大多只听到这个术语用来指代像Sun Ra这样的音乐家，或是塞缪尔·德拉尼（Samuel Delany）或科尔森·怀特黑德（Colson Whitehead）的科幻小说。但在这次会议上，有非洲未来主义艺术家、设计师、程序员、电影制作人、时尚达人、治疗师、瑜伽修行者。大厅里甚至有一位女士在卖自制的辣椒酱。

00:09:29.640 --> 00:09:53.280
<v Neil Drumming>I began to realize that Afrofuturism wasn't an art movement in the way that I thought of, like, Cubism or Impressionism. It wasn't static or rigidly defined. The people at this conference who were so liberally applying the word were doing so because they thought it made their work seems somehow more active and vital. But I didn't understand exactly how.
<v Neil Drumming>我开始意识到，非洲未来主义并不是我想象中那种像立体主义或印象派那样的艺术运动。它不是静态的，也没有僵化的定义。在这次会议上如此随意地使用这个词的人们，是因为他们认为这让他们的作品看起来某种程度上更具行动力和生命力。但我并不完全理解具体是如何运作的。

00:09:53.280 --> 00:10:05.680
<v Neil Drumming>As the term became more ubiquitous and The New York Times used Afrofuturism to talk about Beyonce's wardrobe changes, I was still trying to figure out the essence of the thing. Like, what makes something Afrofuturist?
<v Neil Drumming>随着这个词变得越来越普遍，《纽约时报》甚至用“非洲未来主义”来谈论碧昂丝（Beyonce）的服装变换，我仍然试图弄清它的精髓。就像，是什么让某样东西变得“非洲未来主义”？

00:10:05.680 --> 00:10:31.940
<v Neil Drumming>I called the woman who'd given the keynote address back at that conference in 2015, activist and writer Adrienne Maree Brown. Adrienne told me that if I wanted to see Afrofuturism that I should visit her hometown, Detroit, Michigan-- a post-apocalyptic city, she'd said. The auto industry is nearly gone, the city's infrastructure is crumbling, many people can't afford basic resources like water and light. It's like the setting for some dystopian sci-fi story.
<v Neil Drumming>我给那位在2015年会议上发表主旨演讲的女性打了电话，她是活动家、作家阿德里安娜·玛丽·布朗（Adrienne Maree Brown）。阿德里安娜告诉我，如果我想看非洲未来主义，我就应该去她的家乡——密歇根州的底特律，她称之为“后启示录城市”。汽车工业几乎消失了，城市的基建正在瓦解，许多人买不起水和电等基本资源。这就像某种反乌托邦科幻故事的背景。

00:10:31.940 --> 00:10:57.470
<v Neil Drumming>As soon as I started reading up on the place, I came across this headline in the Detroit Metro Times. "Inside Ingrid LaFleur's Afrofuturist mayoral campaign." Wow. There was a woman running for mayor of Detroit, and the basis of her platform wasn't socialism or libertarianism. It was Afrofuturism. So I flew out there to hang out with her. I got to hear her do some fundraising, give a speech, talk to some potential voters, and we drove around.
<v Neil Drumming>我一读到关于这个地方的报道，就看到了《底特律地铁时报》的这个标题：“深入英格丽德·拉弗勒（Ingrid LaFleur）的非洲未来主义市长竞选”。哇。居然有位女性在竞选底特律市长，而她纲领的基础既不是社会主义也不是自由意志主义。而是非洲未来主义。所以我飞过去和她待了一阵子。我听了她的一些筹款活动，听了演讲，与一些潜在选民交谈，我们还开车到处逛了逛。

00:10:57.470 --> 00:11:00.320
<v Ingrid Lafleur>And see all the different types of architecture here.
<v Ingrid Lafleur>看看这里所有不同类型的建筑。

00:11:00.320 --> 00:11:20.990
<v Neil Drumming>Ingrid is striking, impeccably styled and glamorous, yet still very bohemian, with a shock of gray in her perfectly coiffed hair. She pointed out her favorite landmarks. We visited a compact urban farm where they make something edible called Afrojam. We rode past a spot in Detroit's North End, where George Clinton's band Parliament-Funkadelic played one of his most iconic albums.
<v Neil Drumming>英格丽德令人印象深刻，打扮得无懈可击且时髦，但依然非常波西米亚风格，那一头精心梳理的头发中有着一抹灰色。她指出了她最喜欢的地标。我们参观了一个紧凑的城市农场，在那里他们制作一种名为Afrojam的可食用产品。我们开车经过底特律北区的一个地方，乔治·克林顿（George Clinton）的Parliament-Funkadelic乐队在那里表演了他最标志性的专辑之一。

00:11:20.990 --> 00:11:30.920
<v Ingrid Lafleur>George Clinton did create the Mothership Connection in Detroit, and he performed it here on Oakland Avenue, which is the street that we're on right now.
<v Ingrid Lafleur>乔治·克林顿确实在底特律创造了“母舰连接”，他就在我们现在所在的奥克兰大道上表演了它。

00:11:30.920 --> 00:11:39.310
<v Neil Drumming>Taking a reporter around and bragging about your hometown is what you'd expect from anyone running for mayor. But it wasn't long before Ingrid the Afrofuturist showed up as well.
<v Neil Drumming>带着记者到处炫耀你的家乡是任何竞选市长的人都会做的事。但没过多久，身为“非洲未来主义者”的英格丽德也显露了出来。

00:11:39.310 --> 00:11:58.130
<v Ingrid Lafleur>The mothership is this wonderful symbol of our future space. And for me, it's this reminder of this empowering future space that can-- the possibilities of that and how it's always present.
<v Ingrid Lafleur>母舰是我们未来太空空间的一个美好象征。对我来说，这提醒着我，那个可以带来赋权的未来空间……以及这种可能性的空间，它是如何一直存在的。

00:11:58.130 --> 00:12:28.910
<v Neil Drumming>We were talking about stuff I knew. I grew up listening to Parliament records too, and reading the comics on the album covers. I always thought it was cool, groundbreaking stuff. But when Ingrid talked about it, she scaled it up to something more grand and spiritual and a little esoteric. To be honest, I found her a bit difficult to follow on this and other topics. But with her enthusiasm, she was just fun to listen to. And anyway, I came to Detroit to find out about Afrofuturism, and here she was-- someone super-eager and ready to talk about it, whether or not what she said made sense to me.
<v Neil Drumming>我们谈论的是我知道的东西。我从小也是听着Parliament的唱片长大的，也读过专辑封面上的漫画。我一直觉得那很酷，是很开创性的东西。但当英格丽德谈到它时，她将其提升到了更宏大、更精神、更有一点深奥的地步。老实说，在这一话题和其他话题上，我觉得她有点难以捉摸。但带着她的热情，听她说话本身就很有趣。不管怎样，我来底特律是为了了解非洲未来主义，而这里就有她——一个超级热心且准备好讨论它的人，不管她说的话我是否觉得有道理。

00:12:28.910 --> 00:12:38.410
<v Ingrid Lafleur>But my orientation, even within Afrofuturism, has always been cosmic. I'm a cosmic person.
<v Ingrid Lafleur>但我的导向，即使是在非洲未来主义内部，也一直是宇宙性的。我是一个宇宙性的人。

00:12:38.410 --> 00:12:39.980
<v Neil Drumming>Here she is discussing time.
<v Neil Drumming>这是她在讨论时间。

00:12:39.980 --> 00:12:50.470
<v Ingrid Lafleur>Time can be defined in 20 million different ways. It can go diagonally up, it can go diagonally down, below our feet-- and just like in the universe, right?
<v Ingrid Lafleur>时间可以用2000万种不同的方式定义。它可以对角向上，可以对角向下，在我们脚下——就像在宇宙中一样，对吧？

00:12:50.470 --> 00:12:52.460
<v Neil Drumming>And here she is on the subject of lunch.
<v Neil Drumming>这是她在午餐问题上的观点。

00:12:52.460 --> 00:13:07.740
<v Ingrid Lafleur>Pleasure has to resonate all through the future, through your movement, into your world. A pleasurable experience for me eating-wise would be eating a great green salad.
<v Ingrid Lafleur>快乐必须在整个未来、通过你的运动、进入你的世界中产生共鸣。对我来说，饮食方面的快乐体验就是吃一份很棒的绿色沙拉。

00:13:07.740 --> 00:13:39.850
<v Neil Drumming>I'm going to time travel a little myself here and let you know that Ingrid LaFleur did not win the primary. She never even got on the ballot officially. When I was there, with the election rapidly approaching, she was still sorting out her policy points. She talked to me vaguely about reviving Detroit's creative economy to rival the glory days of Motown and about the city's litter problem. Two days before the primary, she published a more thorough plan that called for, among other things, a universal basic income for Detroiters paid partly in digital currency. I guess that's pretty futuristic.
<v Neil Drumming>我要在这里进行一点时间旅行，告诉你们，英格丽德·拉弗勒没有赢得初选。她甚至从未正式出现在选票上。当我在那里时，选举即将来临，她还在梳理她的政策要点。她模糊地跟我谈到复兴底特律的创意经济以媲美摩城（Motown）的辉煌时代，以及城市的垃圾问题。在初选前两天，她发布了一项更详尽的计划，其中包括为底特律居民提供部分以数字货币支付的全民基本收入。我想那确实相当未来主义。

00:13:39.850 --> 00:14:19.060
<v Neil Drumming>But at the heart of Ingrid's campaign was an idea that I found far more compelling than any specific policy she put forth. In her plan for Detroit, she writes, "In order to manifest true revitalisation, we must consider the histories and oppressions of the black American population. The foundation of every institution, government, police, education, the museum, was built to silence, disallow, displace, and render powerless black Americans. These institutions were never created for black Americans to truly prosper. It's time for a new plan."
<v Neil Drumming>但在英格丽德竞选的核心，是一个我认为比她提出的任何具体政策都更令人信服的理念。在她为底特律制定的计划中，她写道：“为了实现真正的复兴，我们必须考虑到美国黑人人口的历史和压迫。每一个机构的基础——政府、警察、教育、博物馆——都是为了压制、否定、流离失所和剥夺美国黑人的权力而建立的。这些机构从来不是为了让美国黑人真正繁荣而创建的。现在是时候制定一个新计划了。”

00:14:19.060 --> 00:14:33.970
<v Neil Drumming>That's not something you hear from many politicians. But it makes sense here, because Detroit is 80% black. Fixing the city means fixing it for black people. Ingrid sees Black Americans as the protagonists in the story of Detroit, its future and its past.
<v Neil Drumming>这不是你从许多政治家那里听到的声音。但在底特律，这很有道理，因为底特律80%是黑人。修复这座城市意味着为黑人修复它。英格丽德将美国黑人视为底特律故事、其未来和过去的主角。

00:14:33.970 --> 00:14:42.820
<v Ingrid Lafleur>When I was growing up, people loved to say it was, like, 110% black. It was super-black. It was just black.
<v Ingrid Lafleur>当我成长的时候，人们喜欢说它是110%的黑人。它是超级黑的。它就是黑人。

00:14:42.820 --> 00:15:03.310
<v Neil Drumming>This is the other thing about Ingrid. Maybe she's idealizing, I wouldn't know. But she carries a shining vision of Detroit's past with her everywhere she goes. We drove past vacant houses and empty lots. So much of Detroit is falling apart, neglected or abandoned. But underneath it all, she still sees a vibrant city, populated with a thriving black middle class.
<v Neil Drumming>这就是英格丽德的另一面。也许她是在理想化，我不知道。但她带着对底特律过去闪耀的愿景，走到哪里都带着它。我们开车经过空置的房屋和空地。底特律很大一部分正在分崩离析，被忽视或遗弃。但在这一切之下，她仍然看到一座充满活力的城市，有着蓬勃发展的黑人中产阶级。

00:15:03.310 --> 00:15:23.170
<v Ingrid Lafleur>I grew up walking through this park at dusk. There would be people riding bikes, jogging, playing squash at dusk-- like, it's getting dark. And my mother and I, we felt completely comfortable and safe walking through these woods.
<v Ingrid Lafleur>我是在黄昏时分穿过这个公园长大的。黄昏时分会有人骑自行车、慢跑、打壁球——就像，天色渐暗。我和我母亲在穿过这些树林时，感觉非常舒适和安全。

00:15:23.170 --> 00:15:42.730
<v Neil Drumming>In the car, we talked about Afrofuturism. At one point, I told her about the conference that I'd been to, and how by now I'd heard the word Afrofuturism used to describe everything from comic books to condiments. But it wasn't coming into focus for me. I was starting to wonder if it was just a term for putting a positive spin on anything a black person has ever done or will ever do.
<v Neil Drumming>在车里，我们谈到了非洲未来主义。有一次，我告诉她我参加过的那个会议，以及到现在为止，我听过“非洲未来主义”这个词被用来形容从漫画书到调味品的一切东西。但这对我来说并没有变得清晰。我开始怀疑，这是否只是一个给黑人做过或将要做的任何事情加上积极修辞的术语。

00:15:42.730 --> 00:15:46.530
<v Neil Drumming>Like, I was moved by the optimism that seems inherent in Afrofuturism.
<v Neil Drumming>就像，我被非洲未来主义中固有的乐观主义所打动。

00:15:46.530 --> 00:15:46.860
<v Ingrid Lafleur>Yes!
<v Ingrid Lafleur>是的！

00:15:46.860 --> 00:15:52.100
<v Neil Drumming>But I was also like, if it's everything, then what is the point of it?
<v Neil Drumming>但我也觉得，如果它代表一切，那它的意义何在？

00:15:52.100 --> 00:16:31.260
<v Ingrid Lafleur>But that is the point-- imagining a more healthier present and future, and kind of recontextualizing the past so that it's empowering instead of victimizing. It's our way of not only surviving, but it's also a way of healing within the moment because there's constant traumas that we're dealing with-- historical and present day. So it's a way of allowing ourselves to shape-shift, to accommodate whatever comes our way.
<v Ingrid Lafleur>但这就是重点——想象一个更健康的现在和未来，并以某种方式重构过去，使其变得赋权而不是受害。这是我们不仅生存的方式，也是当下治愈的方式，因为我们正在处理持续的创伤——历史和现实的创伤。所以这是一种让我们能够变形以适应任何来临事物的方式。

00:16:31.260 --> 00:16:52.260
<v Neil Drumming>Ingrid eventually dropped me back at my hotel. Earlier, she'd given me a greasy brown bag containing a cookie which I now finally got around to eating. It turned out to be the most delicious gluten-free cookie I had ever tasted. But I was still feeling hungry and a bit confused by our conversation. Whatever bigger point she had been making about Afrofuturism, I was afraid that it had been lost on me.
<v Neil Drumming>英格丽德最终把我送回了酒店。早些时候，她给了我一个油腻的棕色纸袋，里面装着一块饼干，我现在终于有时间吃了。结果那是我吃过的最好吃的无麸质饼干。但我仍然感到饥饿，而且对我们的谈话感到有点困惑。无论她关于非洲未来主义说了什么大道理，我担心我已经错过了。

00:16:52.260 --> 00:17:17.820
<v Neil Drumming>I went looking for a burger and a stiff drink to mull it over. At a bar, over bourbon, I met a guy that I'm going to call Sam, with dark skin and hints of gray in his sideburns that betrayed his boyish face. He was dressed for a night on the town-- light blazer, Burberry shirt, patterned silk scarf. I asked him and another patron, both Detroit residents, if they'd heard of Ingrid LaFleur for Mayor. They hadn't.
<v Neil Drumming>我去找了一个汉堡和一杯烈酒来思考这个问题。在一家酒吧，喝着波本威士忌时，我遇到了一个我姑且称之为山姆（Sam）的家伙，他皮肤黝黑，鬓角带着一丝灰色，背叛了他那张稚嫩的脸。他为了晚上的出游精心打扮——浅色西装外套，Burberry衬衫，印花丝绸围巾。我问他和另一位顾客，他们都是底特律居民，是否听说过英格丽德·拉弗勒竞选市长。他们没听说过。

00:17:17.820 --> 00:17:35.030
<v Neil Drumming>When Sam got up to pay his check after we'd only been talking for a few minutes, he surprised me by asking if I wanted to go with him to check out some of the other spots around town. I wasn't dressed for it and I'd left my recording equipment in my room, but what the hell? Sam said it was OK for me to describe what happened next.
<v Neil Drumming>当山姆聊了没几分钟就起身去结账时，他让我感到惊讶，问我是否想和他一起去看看城里的其他地方。我没为这打扮，而且把录音设备留在了房间里，但管他呢？山姆说我描述接下来发生的事没问题。

00:17:35.030 --> 00:18:00.620
<v Neil Drumming>Sam had a nice car, a Mercedes. It never cost him more than $10 to park it in a lot. He loved that about Detroit-- parking was so cheap. He'd moved here from New York sometime in the late '90s and fallen hard for the city. As we drove, he talked up the real estate. He talked about his job and the IT business he ran out of his home. Things seemed to be going well.
<v Neil Drumming>山姆有一辆好车，一辆奔驰。在停车场停车从来没花过超过10美元。他喜欢底特律的这一点——停车费太便宜了。他90年代末从纽约搬到这里，深深地爱上了这座城市。当我们开车时，他大谈房地产。他谈到了他的工作和他从家里经营的IT业务。事情看起来进展顺利。

00:18:00.620 --> 00:18:13.990
<v Neil Drumming>We met up with Sam's friend, Rick, a bald, bearded, alpha male type in a reddish leather jacket, and followed him in his new BMW to a place called The Griot. It was the type of establishment once commonly referred to as grown and sexy.
<v Neil Drumming>我们见到了山姆的朋友里克（Rick），一个秃顶、蓄着胡须、穿着红色皮夹克的雄性领袖型人物，并跟着他那辆新宝马到了一个叫“The Griot”的地方。那是一类过去常被称为“成熟而性感”的场所。

00:18:13.990 --> 00:18:36.920
<v Neil Drumming>Rick ordered another round of bourbon and some wings and flirted with the bartender. By now it was clear that these guys were looking to meet women. And that was weird because of this thing that Sam had told me earlier while we were in the car together. He'd said that not too long ago, his wife had gotten sick, and he'd taken care of her for a year. And then she died, leaving Sam and their teenage son with only each other.
<v Neil Drumming>里克又点了一轮波本威士忌和一些鸡翅，并和酒保调情。到这时很明显，这些人是想去结识女性。这很奇怪，因为山姆早些时候在车里告诉过我一件事。他说不久前，他妻子病了，他照顾了她一年。然后她去世了，只留下了山姆和他们的十几岁的儿子相依为命。

00:18:36.920 --> 00:18:58.750
<v Neil Drumming>Sam didn't actually want to be doing what we were doing tonight. This is, like, only his second time out as a single man. He didn't feel ready to meet women, to date. But he also felt like it was time. He couldn't wait forever. So he was doing what he felt like he had to do to move forward.
<v Neil Drumming>山姆其实不想做我们今晚正在做的事。这是他作为单身男人第二次出门。他觉得还没准备好去结识女性，去约会。但他同时也觉得是时候了。他不能永远等待。所以他正在做他觉得为了前进必须做的事。

00:18:58.750 --> 00:19:13.280
<v Neil Drumming>Sam and Rick took me to a few different spots that night. But the one that I remember the most was a place called Floods. From the outside, it looked like a common family restaurant. I thought it was closed until we rounded the corner and saw the cluster of people leaving their cars with the valet.
<v Neil Drumming>山姆和里克那晚带我去了几个不同的地方。但我印象最深的是一个叫Floods的地方。从外面看，它看起来像一家普通的家庭餐馆。我以为它关门了，直到我们转过弯，看到一堆人把车交给代客泊车。

00:19:13.280 --> 00:19:44.120
<v Neil Drumming>Inside, Floods was huge. Sam said it was probably about 2,500 square feet. A massive bar separated two rooms. At one point, there was both a band and a DJ playing simultaneously. And wall to wall, there were well-dressed, grown-up black folks dancing and singing along to classic soul, funk, and R&B. It reminded me of how Ingrid had described the Detroit she grew up in and its thriving black middle class. 110% black, she had said. Super-black, just black.
<v Neil Drumming>Floods里面非常大。山姆说可能大约有2500平方英尺。一个巨大的吧台将两个房间隔开。有一个时刻，乐队和DJ同时在演奏。墙内墙外，都是穿着讲究、成熟的黑人们在跳舞，跟着经典灵魂乐、放克和R&B音乐一起歌唱。这让我想起了英格丽德描述她长大的底特律及其蓬勃发展的黑人中产阶级时所说的话。110%黑人，她说。超级黑，就是黑人。

00:19:44.120 --> 00:19:54.240
<v Neil Drumming>I'd been drinking all night. So yeah, I was already a little in my feelings. But I swear, I teared up at the sight of it all.
<v Neil Drumming>我喝了一整晚的酒。所以，是的，我已经有点感性了。但我发誓，看到这一切，我热泪盈眶。

00:19:54.240 --> 00:20:05.530
<v Neil Drumming>Sam hadn't wanted to come to Floods at first because he thought too many people would recognize him. I think he didn't want to be judged for being out on the town after his wife's death. And when we walked in, he seemed a little uncomfortable.
<v Neil Drumming>山姆起初不想来Floods，因为他觉得太多人会认出他。我想他不希望自己在妻子去世后出来消遣而被人评头论足。当我们走进去时，他看起来有点不舒服。

00:20:05.530 --> 00:20:43.400
<v Neil Drumming>We split up for a while, and later he found me standing in a corner at the edge of the dance floor, mesmerized. He pulled out his phone and showed me the number he had just gotten of a beautiful woman who walked in earlier and drawn quite a bit of attention. Earlier in the day, Ingrid had said a bunch of Afrofuturist stuff to me about Detroit, shapeshifting, and reckoning with its past, and healing. I felt like I was seeing some smaller version of that now with Sam. Most days, he was probably just a guy in some pain, struggling to survive this very traumatic thing that happened. But tonight, in spite of that, Sam was imagining a better future and doing something about it.
<v Neil Drumming>我们分开了一会儿，后来他发现我站在舞池边缘的一个角落里，出神地看着。他掏出手机，向我展示了他刚刚要到的一个早些时候进来、吸引了不少注意力的漂亮女人的号码。那天早些时候，英格丽德对我说了一堆关于底特律、变形、审视过去和治愈的非洲未来主义内容。我觉得我现在在山姆身上看到了它的一些微小版本。大多数日子里，他可能只是一个痛苦的人，努力在经历这件非常创伤的事情后生存下来。但今晚，尽管如此，山姆正在想象一个更美好的未来，并为此付诸行动。

00:20:43.400 --> 00:20:58.850
<v Neil Drumming>He smiled, shouted some words of encouragement at me over the music, and waded back into the crowd. His spirits were up. His evening wasn't over.
<v Neil Drumming>他笑了，在音乐声中对我喊了一些鼓励的话，又钻回了人群中。他情绪高涨。他的夜晚还没有结束。

00:20:58.850 --> 00:21:20.410
<v Neil Drumming>[MUSIC - "RIGHT IN" BY BRYCE DETROIT FEATURING COKO BUTTAFLI]
<v Neil Drumming>[音乐 - “RIGHT IN” by BRYCE DETROIT FEATURING COKO BUTTAFLI]

00:21:20.410 --> 00:21:37.040
<v Neil Drumming>Act Two, "Past and Perfect." So there's this idea that occurs over and over in Afrofuturism that, summed up, basically says, the past is always with us. It affects us. It has the power to make us stronger if we learn how to use it properly.
<v Neil Drumming>第二幕，“过去与完美”。所以，非洲未来主义中反复出现的一个理念，总结起来基本就是：过去永远与我们同在。它影响着我们。如果我们学会正确地利用它，它就有能力让我们变得更强大。

00:21:37.040 --> 00:21:50.020
<v Neil Drumming>Some West African cultures represent this idea with the image of what's called the Sankofa bird. It's a bird walking forward but with its head looking backward, meaning no matter what, we carry the past with us into the future.
<v Neil Drumming>一些西非文化用一种被称为“桑科法（Sankofa）鸟”的形象来代表这一理念。这是一只向前走但头向后看的鸟，意味着无论如何，我们都把过去带进了未来。

00:21:50.020 --> 00:22:11.340
<v Neil Drumming>In one of the most well-known Afrofuturist novels, Kindred by Octavia Butler, the main character finds herself traveling back and forth between her normal contemporary life in the present and the world of her ancestors in the Antebellum South, where she's treated like a slave. And the two time periods start to merge. That's fiction. Our next story is told by a woman who went through a version of that in real life.
<v Neil Drumming>在最著名的非洲未来主义小说之一，奥克塔维亚·巴特勒的《希德》（Kindred）中，主角发现自己在现代的日常生活和南北战争前南方祖先的世界之间来回穿梭，在那里她被当作奴隶对待。而这两个时间段开始融合。那是小说。我们接下来的故事由一位在现实生活中经历过类似版本的女性讲述。

00:22:11.340 --> 00:22:30.270
<v Azie Dungey>When I graduated from NYU with a BA in Drama and a mostly unplanned double major in Anthropology, my mother asked me, what are you going to do with that, act in a museum? This seemed especially rude coming from a sociologist, but she had a point. For the first few years of my adult life, that's exactly what I did.
<v Azie Dungey>当我以戏剧学士学位从纽约大学毕业，并意外地获得了人类学辅修学位时，我母亲问我，你打算用那个做什么，在博物馆里表演吗？这对一个社会学家来说显得特别粗鲁，但她说的有道理。在我成年生活的最初几年，我正是这样做的。

00:22:30.270 --> 00:22:54.440
<v Neil Drumming>During those years, Azie Dungey was living in her hometown, a suburb of Washington DC, working as an actor at night in plays for which she was paid almost nothing. And to make some extra cash for Christmas, she took a gig, time-traveling to the least desirable job of the 18th century. She was a slave, or she played one anyway, performing for tourists at George Washington's estate, Mount Vernon, for weekend events called Mount Vernon by Candlelight.
<v Neil Drumming>那些年里，阿兹·邓吉（Azie Dungey）住在她的家乡，华盛顿特区郊区，晚上在薪水微薄的戏剧中担任演员。为了圣诞节赚点外快，她找了一份工作，穿越回到18世纪最不受欢迎的工作。她是一名奴隶，或者说她扮演一名奴隶，在乔治·华盛顿的庄园——弗农山庄（Mount Vernon）为游客表演名为“烛光下的弗农山庄”的周末活动。

00:22:54.440 --> 00:23:31.860
<v Azie Dungey>The halls of the mansion were decked for Christmas. Visitors would walk through, stopping in each room to meet a character and hear their monologue about the season. There was Mrs. Washington, beloved wife, Dr. James Craik, Washington's physician and best bud, Nellie Custis, sweet-natured granddaughter, and me, Caroline Branham, slave. I had a script that I used to talk about Caroline's life. It was short. Besides the odd "I'm a slave at Christmas too, a slave to my husband and kids" joke from 40-year-old white women, people were interested and respectful.
<v Azie Dungey>大宅的走廊装饰着圣诞装饰。游客们走进来，在每个房间停下来见一个角色，听他们讲述关于这个季节的独白。有华盛顿夫人，深爱的妻子；克雷克医生，华盛顿的医生兼密友；内莉·库斯蒂斯，甜美的孙女；还有我，卡罗琳·布兰汉姆（Caroline Branham），奴隶。我有一份用来讲述卡罗琳生活的剧本。这很简短。除了偶尔有40岁的白人女性开玩笑说“我也是圣诞节的奴隶，是我丈夫和孩子的奴隶”外，人们都很感兴趣且尊重。

00:23:31.860 --> 00:24:02.210
<v Azie Dungey>After Christmas, I decided to stay in the job. It started out fine. The winter weather meant the visitors were mostly local and relatively few. I enjoyed my daily walks on the treelined lanes, soft snow falling on my hooded, mulberry-colored cape, as I looked out at the stunning view of the Potomac River. And when it was too cold, I would stay in the library and read through musty old books and primary source letters and notations that helped me understand this distant world I inhabited and the woman I portrayed.
<v Azie Dungey>圣诞节后，我决定留在这份工作中。起初一切都很顺利。冬天的天气意味着游客主要是当地人，人数相对较少。我享受在树木葱郁的小径上散步，柔软的雪花落在我的兜帽桑葚色斗篷上，看着波托马克河令人惊叹的景色。天气太冷的时候，我就待在图书馆，翻阅发霉的旧书、原始资料信件和注释，这些帮助我理解我所居住的这个遥远世界以及我所扮演的那个女人。

00:24:02.210 --> 00:24:57.770
<v Azie Dungey>I was Caroline Branham, a real woman who had been housemaid to Mrs. Washington. I studied Caroline's life and times for months. And every day, when I put on a beautiful crimson dress with tiny yellow and green flowers, I became her. With each eyelid I laced on my corset, I felt myself get closer to her posture, how her body related to the world. With my hair pulled back in two large French braids under a bonnet, I sat on a bench and set my fingers to the work Caroline did-- rows of stitches on shirts and brightly colored embroidery. I could picture her laying down her head at night, next to her husband Peter and their six children-- Wilson, Rachel, Jemima, Leanthe, Polly, and little Peter. I imagined how she hoped that one day they would wake up in a world better than the one she'd been given.
<v Azie Dungey>我是卡罗琳·布兰汉姆，一个曾担任华盛顿夫人女佣的真实女性。我研究了卡罗琳的生活和时代几个月。每天，当我穿上一条带有微小黄绿色花朵的漂亮深红色裙子时，我就变成了她。当我系上紧身胸衣的每一个孔时，我感觉自己更接近她的姿态，她的身体如何与世界相连。头发在帽子下扎成两条巨大的法式辫子，我坐在长凳上，让手指开始卡罗琳做的工作——在衬衫上缝线和刺绣鲜艳的图案。我可以想象她晚上在丈夫彼得和他们的六个孩子——威尔逊、雷切尔、杰米玛、莉安丝、波莉和小彼得——身边躺下时的样子。我想象着她如何希望有一天他们能在一个比她所被给予的世界更好的世界中醒来。

00:24:57.770 --> 00:25:23.950
<v Azie Dungey>Then spring came, and tourists from around the country started pouring into Mount Vernon. My job was to simply be available to the visitors, to talk to them and answer their questions as Caroline. This time, there was no script. And I realized pretty quickly that just being there, existing as a black woman in that place, was a threat to the fantasy of a noble America. I was the stain on George Washington's white house.
<v Azie Dungey>然后春天来了，来自全国各地的游客开始涌入弗农山庄。我的工作只是为游客服务，和他们交谈，以卡罗琳的身份回答他们的问题。这一次，没有剧本。我很快意识到，仅仅待在那里，作为一个黑人女性存在于那个地方，就是对高尚美国幻想的威胁。我是乔治·华盛顿白房子上的污点。

00:25:23.950 --> 00:25:58.740
<v Azie Dungey>And I watched them grapple with this right in front of me-- me, the least confrontational human being you'll ever meet. I mean, it took me until I was 25 years old to tell my mom I did not liked her baked tofu smothered in Kraft barbecue sauce. Some people were so uncomfortable with me that they bumbled through the first random thing that came to their mind. You're a slave? Oh, OK. So are the vegetables in the garden real? Fake vegetables. This is an outdoor garden. They are literally coming out of the ground, and there's a man standing there watering them.
<v Azie Dungey>我看着他们在我的面前努力应对——我，你见过的最不爱争论的人。我的意思是，我直到25岁才告诉母亲我不喜欢她用卡夫烧烤酱炖的烤豆腐。有些人对我很不自在，以至于他们笨拙地胡乱说出脑海中的第一件事。你是奴隶？哦，好的。那花园里的蔬菜是真的吗？假蔬菜。这是一个户外花园。它们确实是从地里长出来的，旁边还有一个男人站在那里浇水。

00:25:58.740 --> 00:26:18.510
<v Azie Dungey>Or they immediately asked questions with obvious answers that they just hadn't thought through. Why don't you go up to Massachusetts and go to school instead of being a slave, one person asked? Right, as soon as I can get rid of this very obvious brown skin, steal a horse and a map that I can't read, I will be on my way.
<v Azie Dungey>或者他们直接问一些有明显答案的问题，而这些问题他们根本没深思过。一个人问，为什么你不去马萨诸塞州上学，而要当奴隶呢？对，只要我能摆脱这非常明显的棕色皮肤，偷一匹马和一张我看不太懂的地图，我就会上路。

00:26:18.510 --> 00:26:36.420
<v Azie Dungey>Or they'd go into immediate denial mode, like the old woman who out of nowhere yelled at me, George Washington had no slaves. I said, yes, he did. She responded, no he didn't. And this went on, back and forth, until her son got embarrassed and pulled her away.
<v Azie Dungey>或者他们会直接进入否定模式，就像那位老妇人，不知从哪里冒出来对我大喊：乔治·华盛顿没有奴隶。我说，是的，他有。她回应说，不，他没有。这来来回回持续了很久，直到她儿子感到尴尬把她拉走。

00:26:36.420 --> 00:26:47.740
<v Azie Dungey>Or they offered another offense as some sort of defense. But the Irish were slaves too. OK, that's not really true, and what the hell does that have to do with me?
<v Azie Dungey>或者他们会提出另一种冒犯来作为某种辩护。但爱尔兰人也是奴隶。好吧，那不是真的，而且那跟我也没半点关系。

00:26:47.740 --> 00:27:11.290
<v Azie Dungey>And of course, there were those who would take the opportunity to teach me instead. You know, slavery was a good industrious life where you got room and board for your work. Mm-hmm-- a one-room cabin they built themselves with eight people sleeping on a dirt floor and a bag of corn meal every week. Room and board.
<v Azie Dungey>当然，还有那些会借此机会教导我的人。你知道，奴隶制是一种勤劳的好生活，你因为劳动而获得了食宿。嗯哼——一个他们自己建造的八个人睡在泥地上的单间小屋，每周发一袋玉米面。食宿。

00:27:11.290 --> 00:27:32.830
<v Azie Dungey>It was just a bizarre situation to be in, playing Caroline. I'd be sitting next to Miss Nellie in the midst of her roleplay, who'd be going on and on about the lovely letters that her dear grandpapa, George Washington, had written her, or all the fancy soirees that she attended. We'd be in the greenhouse with a gorgeous garden outside on an estate that overlooked the Potomac.
<v Azie Dungey>这是一个极其荒诞的情况，扮演卡罗琳。我会坐在内莉小姐旁边，在她的角色扮演中，听她喋喋不休地谈论她亲爱的祖父乔治·华盛顿写给她的可爱信件，或者是她参加的所有华丽社交聚会。我们会在温室里，外面有华丽的花园，庄园俯瞰着波托马克河。

00:27:32.830 --> 00:27:55.720
<v Azie Dungey>I'd literally hear white visitors sigh, listening to Miss Nellie read from her letters, full of nostalgia for this time long gone. They'd talk about how much simpler things were back then, how touching George Washington's letters were, what a fine man, and how lucky Miss Nellie was to live such a charming life. It was charming. I was charmed by it.
<v Azie Dungey>我真的会听到白人游客叹息，听内莉小姐朗读她的信件，对那个早已逝去的时代充满怀旧。他们会谈论过去的生活是多么简单，乔治·华盛顿的信件是多么动人，他是个好人，内莉小姐能过上如此迷人的生活是多么幸运。这确实迷人。我被它迷住了。

00:27:55.720 --> 00:28:32.770
<v Azie Dungey>Then it'd be my turn. Here's what I talked about-- how my husband is rented from Mrs. Washington's relative and how he could be sent back at any time, how I go home to my cabin at night, hoping that none of my children have been sold, that once my friend was whipped so bad her fingers were torn and she couldn't sew for three weeks-- all real stuff from Caroline Branham's life. One time, an old lady just walked up to me while I was talking, hugged me, whispered "I'm sorry," and walked away. Another time a man handed me a $20 bill, so I guess that was reparations.
<v Azie Dungey>然后轮到我了。这是我谈论的内容——我的丈夫是从华盛顿夫人的亲戚那里租来的，他随时可能被送回去；我晚上回到家的小屋，祈祷我的孩子都没有被卖掉；曾经我的朋友被鞭打得手指撕裂，三周都无法缝纫——这些都是卡罗琳·布兰汉姆生活中的真实片段。有一次，一位老太太在我说话时走过来，拥抱了我，低声说“对不起”，然后走开了。另一次，一个男人给了我一张20美元的钞票，我想那算是赔偿吧。

00:28:32.770 --> 00:29:26.890
<v Azie Dungey>In Caroline's day, there were typically no more than 10 white people who lived in the Washington household at any given time. Meanwhile, there were 316 slaves. And who was there to tell their story? Me. I was the only black actor. So I felt like it was up to me to be all 316 people, to research them too, so I could talk about my friend the blacksmith or Delphi, the wool spinner. I learned so much about being a slave that to this day there is space in my brain devoted to this information. Like, I can tell you how to shine silver with horse urine, which herbs to use to make your owner sick but not kill her, how beating a slave and then dousing them with salt water to disinfect the wounds was called pickling. Pickling-- I hate that I know that.
<v Azie Dungey>在卡罗琳的时代，华盛顿家中居住的白人通常不超过10人。与此同时，有316名奴隶。谁在那里讲述他们的故事？我。我是唯一的黑人演员。所以我感觉我有责任成为这316个人的化身，去研究他们，这样我才能谈论我的铁匠朋友或羊毛纺纱工德尔菲。我学习了太多关于成为奴隶的事情，以至于直到今天，我的大脑中仍然留有空间专门存放这些信息。就像，我可以告诉你如何用马尿擦亮银器，用什么草药可以让你的主人生病但又不至于要了她的命，如何鞭打奴隶然后用盐水清洗伤口以防感染被称为“酸渍（pickling）”。酸渍——我恨我知道这些。

00:29:26.890 --> 00:29:59.190
<v Azie Dungey>As my time at Mount Vernon went on, I began to notice there was something that would happen in particular with white men. It was, like, almost despite themselves, they would act out the power dynamic from 1797. I can remember at least five times when a white man of a certain age sat on a bench way too close to me and said something like, I used to be into a black girl, back when I was young. Then he would pause, presumably waiting for my response. What am I supposed to say to that, exactly, thank you?
<v Azie Dungey>随着我在弗农山庄的时间推移，我开始注意到白人男性身上会发生一件特别的事情。他们几乎是不由自主地演绎出1797年的那种权力动态。我记得至少有五次，一位一定年纪的白人坐在我旁边过近的长凳上，说类似的话：我年轻的时候曾经迷恋过一个黑人女孩。然后他会停顿，大概在等我的回应。我到底该对那种话说什么，谢谢你？

00:29:59.190 --> 00:30:27.270
<v Azie Dungey>Once a man got physical. I was walking down the lane on a particularly hot and busy day when a tall, burly white man grabbed my arm, pulled me towards him, and said, show me where you're branded. I was startled and could only get out the words, sir, I haven't been branded. And then he and his wife began laughing heartily. Well, you got it good up here. We're from South Carolina, and they branded them down there. Girl, you got it good.
<v Azie Dungey>有一次有个男人动手动脚。在一个特别炎热繁忙的日子里，我沿着小径走，一个高大壮实的白人抓住了我的胳膊，把我拉向他，说：让我看看你在哪里被烙印了。我吓了一跳，只能说出：先生，我没有被烙印。然后他和他的妻子开始大声嘲笑。好吧，你在这里过得不错。我们来自南卡罗来纳州，他们在那里确实会烙印。女孩，你过得挺好。

00:30:27.270 --> 00:30:51.810
<v Azie Dungey>And then one time, one of the guys I work with, also in costume, wearing a tricorn hat, he and I were walking from the farm back to the mansion, and we decided to take the forest trail. We were having a nice, normal conversation when out of the blue he says, hey, let's re-enact a slave rape. You tear up your clothes a little bit and go running out the forest screaming, and I'll follow behind you pulling up my pants.
<v Azie Dungey>还有一次，我和一起工作的一个同事，他也穿着戏服，戴着三角帽，我们要从农场走回大宅，决定走森林小径。我们正在进行正常的谈话，他突然说：嘿，我们重演一场奴隶强奸吧。你把衣服撕破一点，从森林里尖叫着跑出来，我跟在后面提裤子。

00:30:51.810 --> 00:31:14.070
<v Azie Dungey>He laughed. I didn't say anything. I just sort of tittered. I [BLEEP] tittered. That's an old-timey, fancy way of saying I laughed with the bastard. I laughed because I didn't know what else to do, because I felt completely and utterly powerless.
<v Azie Dungey>他笑了。我没说什么。我只是轻笑了一声。我[哔]轻笑了一声。这是一种老派、做作的说法，意思是我和那个混蛋一起笑了。我笑了，因为我不知道该怎么办，因为我感到彻底且完全的无力。

00:31:14.070 --> 00:31:46.140
<v Azie Dungey>I started calling in sick a lot because I was sick a lot. My stomach would twist and turn. I would sometimes get a choking feeling and cough as if to vomit, but nothing was there. I had terrible headaches, and I couldn't do anything but sleep. And one day it occurred to me that Caroline felt this way too, this exact way. The farm manager who oversaw slaves on the Mansion House Farm noted that Caroline, quote, "complained of a pain in her head and side," and had, quote, "a very dry bad cough."
<v Azie Dungey>我开始经常请病假，因为我真的经常生病。我的胃会翻江倒海。有时会有哽咽感，咳嗽得好像要呕吐，但什么都没有。我有严重的头痛，除了睡觉什么也做不了。有一天我突然意识到，卡罗琳也有这种感觉，一模一样的感觉。负责大宅农场奴隶的农场经理记录道，卡罗琳引用说“抱怨头和侧部疼痛”，并且引用说“咳嗽非常严重”。

00:31:46.140 --> 00:32:04.060
<v Azie Dungey>George Washington's friend, Dr. Craik, is recorded as having treated her for such bad stomach pains that he had to bleed her, give her stomach drops, and prescribe 20 pills for bowel obstruction. My job was to inhabit Caroline's life, but she started inhabiting mine.
<v Azie Dungey>华盛顿的朋友克雷克医生被记录在案，曾因她严重的胃痛而不得不为她放血、滴胃药，并开了20粒肠梗阻药。我的工作是进入卡罗琳的生活，但她开始进入我的生活。

00:32:04.060 --> 00:32:30.550
<v Azie Dungey>I had this weird sensation that I was living in multiple time periods at once, like the Sankofa bird. I started seeing Mount Vernon everywhere in the modern world. An evolutionary psychologist wrote a blog post for Psychology Today, where he claimed that he objectively proved that black women are the least attractive of all races of women with science. This happened in the year of our Lord 2011.
<v Azie Dungey>我有这种奇怪的感觉，我同时生活在多个时间段，就像桑科法鸟。我开始在现代世界各处看到弗农山庄。一位进化心理学家在《今日心理学》上写了一篇博客文章，声称他客观地用科学证明了黑人女性是所有女性种族中最不具吸引力的。这发生在主后2011年。

00:32:30.550 --> 00:32:57.220
<v Azie Dungey>I saw this only a few days after reading Thomas Jefferson's Notes on the State of Virginia for work. Apparently Jefferson-- who, by the way, was known to have slaves with the lightest skin possible-- had come to the same conclusion about my race and gender over 200 years ago. He said that black men prefer white women, and black women are preferred by orangutans. Oh, so that's why I'm single.
<v Azie Dungey>我在为了工作阅读托马斯·杰斐逊的《弗吉尼亚纪事》几天后才看到这个。显然杰斐逊——顺便说一句，他以拥有肤色最浅的奴隶而闻名——在200多年前就得出了关于我的种族和性别的相同结论。他说黑人男性更喜欢白人女性，而黑人女性更受猩猩青睐。哦，所以这就是我单身的原因。

00:32:57.220 --> 00:33:23.060
<v Azie Dungey>Then, Trayvon Martin. I was watching the news one evening about a black boy harassed and pursued by a white Latino man who was convinced that he didn't belong in his neighborhood. And because of this compulsion, the innocent black boy ended up dead. My heart was in my gut. The first thought that came into my head, he didn't have his pass. Pass? I shook it off. No, that can't be right.
<v Azie Dungey>然后是特雷沃恩·马丁（Trayvon Martin）。一天晚上，我看着新闻，一个黑人男孩被一个白人拉美裔男人骚扰和追逐，那个男人坚信他不属于那个社区。因为这种强迫症，那个无辜的黑人男孩最终死了。我的心沉到了谷底。我脑子里浮现出的第一个想法是：他没带通行证。通行证？我摇了摇头。不，那不对。

00:33:23.060 --> 00:33:55.400
<v Azie Dungey>When a slave left their plantation, they had to carry a pass from their master or overseer saying where they were going and when they were expected to return. Not having your pass, or your free papers if you were free, was dangerous. At any time, any white person could stop you and demand to see your pass. If you didn't produce it, you were thrown in jail. Having black skin alone was enough to make you suspicious, and a white stranger could be the judge and jury of your fate. Trayvon Martin didn't have his pass.
<v Azie Dungey>当奴隶离开种植园时，他们必须携带一张来自主人或监工的通行证，说明他们要去哪里以及预计何时返回。没有通行证，或者如果你是自由人，没有自由证，是很危险的。任何白人随时都可以拦住你，要求看你的通行证。如果你拿不出来，就会被扔进监狱。仅凭黑皮肤就足以让你成为怀疑对象，而一个白人陌生人可以成为你命运的法官和陪审团。特雷沃恩·马丁没有带通行证。

00:33:55.400 --> 00:34:09.630
<v Azie Dungey>There's a thing white people say to black people all the time. I've had it said to me. Why don't you just get over it-- "it" being slavery and all historical oppression. Why don't you just get over it, they say. It's in the past.
<v Azie Dungey>白人经常对黑人说一句话。别人也对我这么说过。为什么你不忘掉它——“它”指奴隶制和所有历史压迫。为什么你不干脆忘掉它，他们说。那都是过去的事了。

00:34:09.630 --> 00:34:33.440
<v Azie Dungey>What I would say to white people in response is that you're not over the past. Every year you flock from all over the country to Mount Vernon and other places like it, to monuments and museums and reenactments. There's President's Day and the fireworks that blast over George Washington's house every 4th of July. There are the Confederate flags and statues of Robert E. Lee that you don't want taken down.
<v Azie Dungey>我想对白人回应的是，你并没有忘掉过去。每年你们从全国各地涌向弗农山庄和其他类似的地方，涌向纪念碑、博物馆和重演活动。有总统日，有每年7月4日在乔治·华盛顿的故居上空炸响的烟花。有你们不想拆除的南方邦联旗帜和罗伯特·李（Robert E. Lee）的雕像。

00:34:33.440 --> 00:34:45.570
<v Azie Dungey>No, I can't get over it. And neither can you. None of us can.
<v Azie Dungey>不，我忘不掉。你们也忘不掉。我们谁都忘不掉。

00:34:45.570 --> 00:34:56.280
