00:42:58.360 --> 00:43:25.060
<v Neil Drumming>Act Four, "Childhood's End." Looking into Afrofuturism over the past few months, I found myself thinking about time and space a lot. Time, because so many of these stories involve people thinking about the past or the future or seeing the past and the future in the here and now. And space, because all of that time travel is cued by very specific places-- Detroit, or Mount Vernon, or Philadelphia, or wherever.
<v Neil Drumming>第四幕，“童年的终结”。在过去的几个月里探究非洲未来主义，我发现自己经常在思考时间和空间。时间，是因为这些故事中有太多涉及人们思考过去或未来，或者在此时此地审视过去和未来。空间，是因为所有的那些时间旅行都是由非常具体的地点所引发的——底特律、或弗农山庄、或费城，等等。

00:43:25.060 --> 00:43:59.530
<v Neil Drumming>During those same months, I also became sort of obsessed with these two videos that I saw on YouTube. I couldn't help connecting them in my mind because for me, they were both about a particularly precious intersection of time and space. The time is that period when children have left school for the day but haven't yet made it home. And the space is that distance, however many blocks, however many miles it takes those kids to walk from one to the other. In other words, these two videos are about those moments when kids are out in the world on their own and the adults they bump into.
<v Neil Drumming>在同样的几个月里，我也变得有点沉迷于我在YouTube上看到的这两个视频。我忍不住在脑海中把它们联系起来，因为对我来说，它们都关于时间与空间一个特别珍贵的交集。时间是孩子们放学后但还没到家之前的那段时间。空间是那段距离，无论多少街区，无论多少英里，孩子们从那里走到家里所需的距离。换句话说，这两个视频是关于孩子们独自在世界徘徊，以及他们偶遇的成年人的时刻。

00:43:59.530 --> 00:44:17.950
<v Neil Drumming>You may have seen the first video. It takes place in Atlantic City. Two black teenagers in sweatpants and sneakers are fighting in the middle of a residential street on their way home from school when a slightly older black man, Ibn Ali Miller, emerges from camera right and puts an end to the fight with a stern but passionate talking to.
<v Neil Drumming>你们可能看过第一个视频。它发生在大西洋城。两个穿着运动裤和运动鞋的黑人少年在放学回家的路上在一处居民区街道中间打架，这时稍微年长一点的黑人男子伊本·阿里·米勒（Ibn Ali Miller）从镜头右侧出现，用严厉但充满激情的一番话结束了这场争斗。

00:44:17.950 --> 00:44:33.780
<v Neil Drumming>The clip went viral. Miller was honored by the city. Steve Harvey had him on his couch. I saw it for the first time on my Facebook feed this past winter, and then viewed it on YouTube many times since. It does not disappoint. It gets better every time I watch it.
<v Neil Drumming>这个片段在网上疯传。米勒受到了城市的表彰。史蒂夫·哈维（Steve Harvey）请他上了沙发。我是在去年冬天第一次在我的Facebook动态上看到它的，之后在YouTube上看了很多次。它没让人失望。我每次看都会觉得更好。

00:44:33.780 --> 00:44:38.260
<v Neil Drumming>[KIDS TAUNTING, LAUGHING]
<v Neil Drumming>[孩子们嘲讽、大笑]

00:44:38.260 --> 00:44:47.740
<v Neil Drumming>When it begins, the two boys grapple and flail at each other sloppily-- one tall, one chubby, both defiant. Other kids crowd around, recording with their cell phones, egging them on.
<v Neil Drumming>视频开始时，两个男孩笨拙地扭打在一起——一个高，一个胖，都充满挑衅。其他孩子围在周围，用手机录制，怂恿他们。

00:44:47.740 --> 00:44:50.230
<v Kid>Square Up, go ahead, Brown.
<v Kid>对峙，来吧，布朗。

00:44:50.230 --> 00:44:52.720
<v Kid>[KIDS TAUNTING, LAUGHING]
<v Kid>[孩子们嘲讽、大笑]

00:44:52.720 --> 00:45:02.080
<v Neil Drumming>Then, seemingly out of nowhere, Miller bops into frame, his hands in his pockets, wearing work boots and a knit cap. He immediately starts to call out the kids who are recording.
<v Neil Drumming>然后，看似凭空，米勒走进了画面，手插在口袋里，穿着工装靴和针织帽。他立刻开始斥责那些正在录像的孩子们。

00:45:02.080 --> 00:45:11.900
<v Ibn Ali Miller>Everybody on your phones, though, all y'all, y'all the real cowards. Record that too. Everybody on the phone, record that. Everybody record on the phone, real coward.
<v Ibn Ali Miller>所有拿着手机的，你们所有这些，你们才是真正的懦夫。把那也录下来。所有在手机上录像的，录下来。所有人在手机上录像，真正的懦夫。

00:45:11.900 --> 00:45:15.790
<v Neil Drumming>Cowards, Miller calls them. Then he turns his attention to the two boys in the center.
<v Neil Drumming>懦夫，米勒这样称呼他们。然后他将注意力转向中间的两个男孩。

00:45:15.790 --> 00:45:25.480
<v Ibn Ali Miller>Listen, they all laughing, little brother. But you really upset. And you really upset. You more mad than he is, I can see it in your face. So you fighting for a reason. He only fighting you because you want to fight him.
<v Ibn Ali Miller>听着，他们都在笑，小兄弟。但你真的很沮丧。你也很沮丧。你比他更生气，我能从你的脸上看出来。所以你是为了一个理由在打架。他只是因为你想打才打你。

00:45:25.480 --> 00:45:30.160
<v Kid>No, he came to me. He touched me. I don't-- [BLEEP], I don't even know.
<v Kid>不，是他来找我。他碰了我。我不知道……[哔]，我甚至不知道。

00:45:30.160 --> 00:45:30.880
<v Ibn Ali Miller>So you defending yourself?
<v Ibn Ali Miller>所以你在为自己辩护？

00:45:30.880 --> 00:45:31.420
<v Kid>Yeah.
<v Kid>是的。

00:45:31.420 --> 00:45:44.330
<v Neil Drumming>Miller listens. And then he tells the boys something they probably hadn't considered-- that they were most likely tricked into fighting by someone who thought it would be entertaining. And now all of their so-called friends are recording them go at it.
<v Neil Drumming>米勒听着。然后他告诉这两个男孩他们可能没考虑过的事情——他们极有可能是被某个觉得这会很有趣的人骗去打架的。现在他们所有所谓的“朋友”都在录下他们打斗的过程。

00:45:44.330 --> 00:45:50.680
<v Ibn Ali Miller>Look around. Who ill-advised you? Who told you wrong? Who told you wrong? You want to answer that? I'm saying, who told you wrong, little bro?
<v Ibn Ali Miller>看看周围。谁给你出的烂主意？谁教错了你？谁教错了你？你想回答吗？我是说，谁教错了你，小兄弟？

00:45:50.680 --> 00:46:08.920
<v Ibn Ali Miller>And the only reason I'm saying this to all y'all, yo, because you're almost men. All y'all, yo. You're almost men. Y'all ain't kids no more. Y'all girls ain't little girls no more. Look, yo. Y'all men, yo. Y'all men, yo. 14, 15, 16, 17-- y'all men. Start acting like it, yo. We ain't gonna get nowhere like this, yo.
<v Ibn Ali Miller>我之所以对你们所有人说这些，是因为你们快要成男人了。你们所有人。你们不再是小孩子了。你们女孩也不再是小女孩了。看着，喂。你们是男人，喂。你们是男人。14、15、16、17岁——你们是男人。开始像个男人一样行事吧，喂。我们这样下去哪儿也去不了，喂。

00:46:08.920 --> 00:46:30.300
<v Neil Drumming>We ain't going to get nowhere like this, he says. Here, what Miller does that I love is steer the conversation away from personal grievances toward something bigger. He tries to tell these kids that they are part of a community. He tells them that their actions have consequences and that those consequences only get more serious as they get older. Without condescending to them, Miller talks to them about their futures.
<v Neil Drumming>我们这样下去哪儿也去不了，他说。米勒做的一件我最喜欢的事情就是将谈话从个人恩怨引导向更大的层面。他试图告诉这些孩子他们是一个共同体的一部分。他告诉他们，他们的行为有后果，而且随着年龄增长，这些后果只会更严重。没有对他们屈尊俯就，米勒与他们谈论他们的未来。

00:46:30.300 --> 00:46:42.970
<v Ibn Ali Miller>You think it's a game out here? Ain't no game out here. It's real out here, little bro. Word up. Don't make your parents look like this, yo. And if y'all live around here, y'all live somewhere good. Don't mess that up, bro.
<v Ibn Ali Miller>你以为这只是游戏吗？这可不是游戏。这可是现实，小兄弟。听着。别让你父母看起来像这样，喂。如果你们住在这附近，你们住的是个好地方。别把它搞砸了，兄弟。

00:46:42.970 --> 00:46:59.680
<v Neil Drumming>He's no longer just trying to get them to save face or avoid humiliation. He's warning the boys that as they become men, young black men, the world will become even more dangerous for them. Miller won't leave until the two shake hands. They don't want to at first. Only the chubby kid extends his hand.
<v Neil Drumming>他不再只是试图让他们保住面子或避免屈辱。他是在警告男孩们，随着他们成长为男人、年轻的黑人男子，世界对他们来说会变得更加危险。米勒不打算离开，直到两人握手言和。起初他们不想。只有胖男孩伸出了手。

00:46:59.680 --> 00:47:04.060
<v Ibn Ali Miller>Y'all gonna shake hands or I'm not leaving, bro. I'm not leaving, bro. Shake hands, bro.
