<v Neil Drumming>另一个孩子没有握。胖男孩缩回了手。

00:47:08.910 --> 00:47:14.790
<v Ibn Ali Miller>Put your hand back out, bro. He gonna shake it this time, I promise you. Put your hand back out, bro. He gonna shake it, bro.
<v Ibn Ali Miller>把手伸回去，兄弟。我保证他这次会握的。把手伸回去，兄弟。他会握的，兄弟。

00:47:14.790 --> 00:47:23.530
<v Neil Drumming>He does.
<v Neil Drumming>他握了。

00:47:23.530 --> 00:47:52.900
<v Neil Drumming>It's actually difficult for me to express how moved I was by this video. The beauty of it is that even while Miller is insisting that these teenagers become responsible for themselves, his actions demonstrate that they are not necessarily alone, that there is compassion and wisdom and guidance available to them. It was like a window into a world where people who don't know each other still give a damn, a window that I can rewind and rewatch and forward to my friends to click Like. It was at once exhilarating and comforting.
<v Neil Drumming>实际上，我很难表达我对这个视频有多感动。它的美妙之处在于，即使米勒坚持让这些少年对自己的行为负责，他的行为也证明了他们并非孤身一人，同情、智慧和指引是他们可以获取的。这就像是一扇通往某种世界的窗户，那里的人们即使互不相识也仍然关心彼此；这是一扇我可以倒带、重看，并转发给朋友点击“点赞”的窗户。它既让人兴奋又让人安慰。

00:47:52.900 --> 00:48:22.800
<v Neil Drumming>I rode that high for a week, maybe two. Then another video popped up in my Facebook feed. This one is shakier and from a low angle, obviously recorded surreptitiously. The view is of the intersection of Bedford Avenue and Campus Road, Midwood High School on one side of the street, Brooklyn College is on the other. I recognized it immediately because I used to live 10 minutes away. There's still snow on the ground and the cell phone is buffeted about by wind.
<v Neil Drumming>我沉浸在那阵兴奋中持续了一周，也许两周。然后另一个视频出现在了我的Facebook动态上。这个视频镜头更晃，且角度很低，显然是偷偷录下的。视野是贝德福德大道（Bedford Avenue）和校园路（Campus Road）的交叉口，一边是米德伍德高中（Midwood High School），另一边是布鲁克林学院（Brooklyn College）。我立刻认出了它，因为我曾经住在10分钟路程外。地上还有雪，手机镜头被风吹得乱晃。

00:48:22.800 --> 00:48:50.120
<v Neil Drumming>Its camera wobbles up behind two uniformed police officers walking slowly down Campus Road. The one on the left has both hands in his pockets. The one on the right has his baton already drawn. A handful of black teenagers walk obstinately before them, headed away toward Flatbush Avenue. There's no way to know for sure what happened before the video started rolling. There were, most likely, the beginnings of a snowball fight. But it's unclear who was unhappy about that.
<v Neil Drumming>镜头蹒跚地跟在两名缓慢走在校园路上的穿制服警察身后。左边的那个双手插在口袋里。右边的那个已经拔出了警棍。几个黑人少年正固执地走在他们前面，朝弗拉特布什大道（Flatbush Avenue）走去。无法确定视频开始录制前发生了什么。极有可能是打雪仗的开始。但不清楚是谁对那感到不满。

00:48:50.120 --> 00:49:07.820
<v Neil Drumming>The police were not called. They were already onsite. In the footage, one of the cops says, come on, let's go, though the kids are already going. The cop on the right reaches out. It looks like he shoves a boy in a pink hoodie. The boy flinches, snapping his head around.
<v Neil Drumming>警察不是被叫来的。他们就在现场。镜头中，其中一名警察说：来吧，走吧，尽管孩子们已经在走了。右边的警察伸出手。看起来他推了一个穿着粉色卫衣的男孩。男孩畏缩了一下，猛地转过头。

00:49:07.820 --> 00:49:19.670
<v Neil Drumming>Stop touching me, he says. The cop responds with, what are you going to do about it-- which is obnoxious and a stupid question. But that doesn't stop him from asking it at least five more times, like a kid taunting another kid.
<v Neil Drumming>别碰我，他说。警察回应道：你要干什么——这很讨厌，是个愚蠢的问题。但他还是坚持问了至少五次，就像一个孩子在嘲弄另一个孩子。

00:49:19.670 --> 00:49:26.480
<v Cop>What are you going to do about it? What are you going to do about it? What are you going to do about it? What are you going to do about it? What are you going to do about it?
<v Cop>你要干什么？你要干什么？你要干什么？你要干什么？你要干什么？

00:49:26.480 --> 00:49:47.630
<v Neil Drumming>At one point, one of the teenagers fires back, that's why we out. It sounds bad ass. But if you think about it, the kid is saying, we're walking away because we can't do anything about it. And we all know that. Another kid in the procession kneels down to pick up some snow. With his left hand, the cop on the right pulls his taser out of his holster and barks--
<v Neil Drumming>在某个时刻，其中一名少年回嘴：这就是为什么我们要走。这听起来很酷。但如果你仔细想想，那个孩子是在说：我们走开是因为我们什么也做不了。我们都知道这一点。行列中的另一个孩子跪下来抓起一些雪。左手拿着警棍的警察从枪套里掏出泰瑟枪（Taser），并咆哮着——

00:49:47.630 --> 00:49:51.740
<v Cop>Pick that [BLEEP] up again. Pick that [BLEEP] up again.
<v Cop>再捡起那个[哔]东西试试。再捡起那个[哔]东西试试。

00:49:51.740 --> 00:50:03.320
<v Neil Drumming>Pick that [BLEEP] up again. Rock, paper, scissors, snowball, taser. The kids keep walking away. The cop yells, pick that [BLEEP] up again, one more time, and follows with--
<v Neil Drumming>再捡起那个[哔]东西试试。石头、剪刀、布，打雪仗，泰瑟枪。孩子们继续走开。警察大喊：再捡起那个[哔]东西试试，又来了一次，接着说——

00:50:03.320 --> 00:50:05.330
<v Cop>You want to ride the lightning?
<v Cop>你想尝尝被电击的滋味吗？

00:50:05.330 --> 00:50:23.720
<v Neil Drumming>Do you want to ride the lightning? The video ends as the kids cross the street to safety and the cops stop on the corner. I know. I know it seems like you can see worse, more unjust things happen to black people on YouTube just about any day of the week. Here, fortunately, no one was hurt.
<v Neil Drumming>你想尝尝被电击的滋味吗？视频在孩子们横穿街道去到安全地带时结束，警察停在路口。我知道。我知道这看起来好像你几乎每天在YouTube上都能看到针对黑人更糟糕、更不公正的事情发生。在这里，幸运的是，没人受伤。

00:50:23.720 --> 00:50:54.760
<v Neil Drumming>But this video was still hard to watch, especially having seen how Ibn Ali Miller talked those kids down in Atlantic City. Whereas Miller was trying to get the teenagers to think a little bit more like adults, the cop in this video sounded like a teenager himself, bullying and provoking. What's worse, he almost seemed to be enjoying it. It's hard to imagine he cared anything at all about what happened to those kids after they deserted that corner.
<v Neil Drumming>但这个视频仍然很难看，尤其是看过伊本·阿里·米勒在大西洋城的视频中如何开导那些孩子之后。米勒试图让少年们像成年人一样多思考一点，而这个视频里的警察听起来像个少年自己，欺凌和挑衅。更糟的是，他看起来甚至很享受这一切。很难想象他会关心这些孩子离开那个角落后会怎样。

00:50:54.760 --> 00:51:24.110
<v Neil Drumming>I told my friends about this video too. I sent the link around. It wasn't as popular a clip as the one with Miller. I read everything I could about it. I found out that the kids most likely went to Midwood High School. And a few weeks later, I went to Midwood High School and stood on the corner of Bedford Avenue and Campus Road. I wanted to find someone who had witnessed the incident. I was angry on behalf of those young men, and I wanted to know how they felt. I found one of them immediately, and then the others found me.
<v Neil Drumming>我也告诉了朋友关于这个视频的事。我发了链接。它不如米勒的那个片段那么受欢迎。我读了关于它的所有报道。我发现这些孩子很有可能就读于米德伍德高中。几周后，我去了米德伍德高中，站在贝德福德大道和校园路的路口。我想找到一个目击事件的人。我为那些年轻人感到愤怒，我想知道他们的感受。我立刻找到了其中一个，然后其他人找到了我。

00:51:24.110 --> 00:51:25.260
<v Neil Drumming>Wait, which one were you?
<v Neil Drumming>等等，你是哪一个？

00:51:25.260 --> 00:51:26.760
<v Kid>Me? I was the black kid.
<v Kid>我？我是那个黑人小孩。

00:51:26.760 --> 00:51:28.380
<v Neil Drumming>Well, they were all black kids.
<v Neil Drumming>好吧，他们都是黑人小孩。

00:51:28.380 --> 00:51:30.670
<v Kid>I was the blackest one, the tallest one.
<v Kid>我是最黑的那个，最高的那个。

00:51:30.670 --> 00:51:53.060
<v Neil Drumming>They're in tapered gray sweats and colorful napsacks. They told me they came to this spot almost every day, bought chicken and fries and rice, slap boxed with each other, kicked it with girls, and waited for the rest of their crew to go play basketball down the block. They wanted to know what my microphone was for and if they could get money and girls from being on the radio, and if I could buy them lunch.
<v Neil Drumming>他们穿着紧身灰运动裤和彩色背包。他们告诉我，几乎每天都来这个地方，买鸡肉、薯条和米饭，互相打闹，和女孩们混在一起，等待他们小团队里的其他人去街区那边打篮球。他们想知道我的麦克风是做什么的，上广播能不能拿到钱和结识女孩，以及我能不能给他们买午饭。

00:51:53.060 --> 00:52:13.970
<v Neil Drumming>I asked them what they were doing on the day that the video was recorded. One of them said he was cutting class. Others said they weren't. I asked them why they think the cops came, since their high school has its own security. One of them said that some other kids across the street were throwing snow and the cops thought it was us. One of the boys said it was them. But still, it was only snowballs.
<v Neil Drumming>我问他们视频录制那天在做什么。其中一个说他在逃课。其他人说没有。我问他们为什么认为警察会来，因为他们的中学有自己的保安。其中一个说街对面的其他孩子在扔雪球，警察以为是我们。其中一个男孩说是他们。但归根结底，那只是雪球。

00:52:13.970 --> 00:52:16.440
<v Kid>We were just being kids. We was being kids, chilling.
<v Kid>我们只是像孩子一样。我们只是孩子，在闲逛。

00:52:16.440 --> 00:52:19.050
<v Kid>Yeah, and they was just wilding out.
<v Kid>是的，他们只是在发疯。

00:52:19.050 --> 00:52:20.950
<v Kid>Even though I was snowballing. Even though I was snowballing.
<v Kid>尽管我当时在玩雪球。尽管我当时在玩雪球。

00:52:20.950 --> 00:52:24.030
<v Kid>Yeah, we were just doing what we was doing. And we were just having fun.
<v Kid>是的，我们只是在做我们的事，我们只是在玩得开心。

00:52:24.030 --> 00:52:37.850
<v Neil Drumming>I asked if they were scared when it happened. A couple of 16-year-olds, cooler than I had ever been at that age, said, no, not really. A 14-year-old said, emphatically, of course. He was 6' 2, my height.
<v Neil Drumming>我问他们发生那事时是否感到害怕。几个16岁的少年，比我那个年纪时酷得多，说，不，没真的怕。一个14岁的男孩强调说，当然怕。他有6英尺2英寸高，和我一样高。

00:52:37.850 --> 00:52:59.070
<v Neil Drumming>I was surprised at how tall some of the boys were, none of them older than 16. I was also surprised at how not angry they seemed. I grew up about 45 minutes from here in a similar neighborhood in Queens, not so long ago, I thought. But when I asked them why they thought this had happened to them at all, one of the older boys talked to me like he was explaining why humans need food. The cop was white.
<v Neil Drumming>我很惊讶其中一些男孩长得那么高，没一个超过16岁。我也很惊讶他们看起来一点也不愤怒。我想，我长大的地方离这大约45分钟路程，在皇后区一个类似的社区，没多久前。但当我问他们为什么认为这事会发生在他们身上时，其中一个大一点的男孩跟我说话的口吻就像在解释为什么人类需要食物一样。警察是白人。

00:52:59.070 --> 00:53:03.760
<v Kid>You know they don't like black people. And I thought-- I was like, oh, we about to [IMITATING TASER].
<v Kid>你知道他们不喜欢黑人。我想……我当时就像，哦，我们要[模仿泰瑟枪声]。

00:53:03.760 --> 00:53:05.370
<v Kid>Yeah, we about to get tased.
<v Kid>是的，我们要被电击了。

00:53:05.370 --> 00:53:42.340
<v Neil Drumming>Maybe this is just me, but I look back on walks home from school as my first real flirtations with independence, a sacred time and space between my teachers' rules and my parents' restrictions. The kids in that video, this was their school. This was their neighborhood. Most of the ones I talked to live only four or five blocks away. As far as they were concerned, this was their corner. I can't imagine what it would feel like to be firmly in your time and space and run up against a cop with a taser, threatening to electrocute you if you don't put down that snowball.
<v Neil Drumming>也许这只是我的想法，但我回顾放学回家的路，把它看作我第一次真正的独立启蒙，在老师的规矩和父母的限制之间有一段神圣的时间和空间。那个视频里的孩子们，那是他们的学校。这是他们的社区。我交谈过的绝大多数孩子只住四五个街区远。据他们所知，这是他们的地盘。我无法想象坚定地处于自己的时间和空间里，却遇到一个拿着泰瑟枪的警察，威胁如果你不放下雪球就电击你，会是什么感觉。

00:53:42.340 --> 00:54:05.850
<v Neil Drumming>What's crazier to me is that I've walked through this intersection at Bedford Avenue and Campus Road, past the high school, and through the Brooklyn College campus many times in the last few years. Beneath its landmark-gilded clocktower, Brooklyn College could be a campus quad from a corny 1950s film, except teeming with students from cultures all over New York. Something about it practically screams "welcome, things only get better from here."
<v Neil Drumming>更让我疯狂的是，过去几年里，我多次走过贝德福德大道和校园路的那个路口，走过高中，穿过布鲁克林学院的校园。布鲁克林学院在那座地标性的镀金钟楼之下，可能像是来自一部庸俗的1950年代电影的校园广场，只是挤满了来自纽约各地的文化背景的学生。关于它的一些东西几乎在尖叫着“欢迎，从这里开始，一切只会变得更好”。

00:54:05.850 --> 00:54:23.980
<v Neil Drumming>The young black men in that video came out of their high school that day and stood at the edge of that promise. What could be the next great space and time in their lives was a college literally across the street, but they were shooed away, told they didn't belong. As far as metaphors go, this one couldn't be less ambiguous.
<v Neil Drumming>视频里那些年轻的黑人男子那天走出高中，站在那个承诺的边缘。他们生活中下一个伟大的时间和空间可能就是 literalmente 马路对面的大学，但他们被赶走了，被告知他们不属于这里。作为隐喻，这一点也不含糊。

00:54:23.980 --> 00:54:38.410
<v Neil Drumming>But none of this seemed to bother the boys, or at least not that they let on. This was not the first time that they had been made to feel unwelcome so close to home and school. Yet they weren't festering with resentment. They weren't seeing the tragedy in it that I was seeing.
<v Neil Drumming>但这似乎并没有困扰这些男孩，或者至少他们没有表现出来。这不是他们第一次在离家和学校这么近的地方感到不受欢迎。然而，他们并没有怀着怨恨。他们并没有看到我所看到的其中的悲剧。

00:54:38.410 --> 00:55:00.620
<v Neil Drumming>After I finished talking to them, I went across the street to find the Brooklyn College professor who had recorded the incident. Alex Vitale teaches sociology and also happens to run the school's policing and social justice project. Leaving work that day, he'd seen the two cops descend on Bedford Avenue on Campus Road, followed them, and it was his recording that ended up on YouTube, as well as a few local news websites.
<v Neil Drumming>谈话结束后，我穿过马路去寻找那个录制事件的布鲁克林学院教授。亚历克斯·维塔莱（Alex Vitale）教授社会学，也恰好负责学校的警务和社会正义项目。那天离开工作场所时，他看到两名警察降临到贝德福德大道和校园路，就跟了上去，而他的录音最终出现在了YouTube以及一些当地新闻网站上。

00:55:00.620 --> 00:55:25.410
<v Neil Drumming>We talked briefly about how repeated incidents like this could affect the boys' psyches. He agreed that no matter how cool they seem now, over time, their hopes for their own futures could possibly darken. But then Alex asked me how old I was. When I told him 43, he said that I had missed the height of the stop and frisk era. I was not a teenager or even a young man in New York City when unprompted police harassment was at its worst.
<v Neil Drumming>我们简短地谈论了像这样反复发生的事件会如何影响男孩们的心理。他同意，无论他们现在看起来多酷，随着时间的推移，他们对自己未来的希望可能会变暗。但随后亚历克斯问我多大了。当我告诉他43岁时，他说我错过了“拦截搜身”（stop and frisk）时代的巅峰。我在纽约市警察骚扰最严重的时候不是少年，甚至不是年轻人。

00:55:25.410 --> 00:56:06.110
<v Neil Drumming>So the taser incident outraged me. But what these kids had seen their older brothers, sisters, and in some cases parents go through had conditioned them to be a bit more stoic. It stung a little bit to have this white academic tell me that I couldn't quite relate to a young black man's experience. I guess when I first approached those boys, a part of me hoped that, like Ibn Ali Miller in the Atlantic City video, I would just roll up out of nowhere and know everything about their world that they know, plus some more. Instead, I was just another adult, taking their time and invading their space.
<v Neil Drumming>所以那个泰瑟枪事件让我愤怒。但这些孩子亲眼目睹了他们的哥哥、姐姐，在某些情况下还有父母所经历的事情，这让他们变得更加坚忍。让这个白人学者告诉我我无法完全理解年轻黑人的经历，让我有点刺痛。我想，当我第一次接近那些男孩时，我的一部分希望，就像大西洋城视频里的伊本·阿里·米勒一样，我会突然出现，并且了解他们世界里的一切，甚至更多。相反，我只是另一个大人，占用了他们的时间，侵犯了他们的空间。

00:56:06.110 --> 00:56:25.340
<v Neil Drumming>[MUSIC - "GOOD TO YOUR EARHOLE" BY FUNKADELIC]
<v Neil Drumming>[音乐 - “GOOD TO YOUR EARHOLE” by FUNKADELIC]

